></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="5" rect="595.276001,68.007294,779.276001,160.007294"
/></text
><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20250515125402+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="672eee56-1050-4f29-ab26-3731509e9a71" date="D:20250515125424+10'00'" name="38d54101-6b68-447d-ad89-73799f66d0df" icon="Comment" page="5" rect="332.750183,136.007294,356.750183,160.007294" subject="Sticky Note" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>In summary, our 8-year&#xD;longitudinal study of Australian elite&#xD;rowers highlights that chest wall and lumbar injuries are the&#xD;greatest challenge and focus areas for future injury prevention for&#xD;the rowing athlete and clinician. Cross-training&#xD;modalities and&#xD;policy decisions can significantly influence injury rates. Illness is an&#xD;underappreciated burden in rowers.&#xD;&#xD;&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:bold;font-style:normal"
>How might it impact on clinical practice in the future?&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>►► </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Priority areas for injury prevention in rowing: chest wall&#xD;injuries (particularly among female rowing athletes) and low&#xD;back pain (particularly among male rowers).&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>►► </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Coaches and athletes should be aware that road cycling for&#xD;cross training is associated with traumatic injury and consider&#xD;this in their training prescription.&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>►► </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Team clinicians should consider illness prevention&#xD;programmes in rowers.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="5" rect="595.276001,68.007294,779.276001,160.007294"
/></text
